WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Mira (narration): Glenn Broder trains professionals to keep their name on the final decision even when AI drafts it.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Glenn: They have to be willing to sign their name to the final product. If they understand that that is their responsibility, that's where they keep the control and the human judgment.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Mira (narration): That's Glenn, creator of Human Judgment in the Age of AI and Learn AI Like a Pro, who builds training that treats AI as a tool while keeping the human signature on every output. This is Nerra Voices from the Nerra Network, the first podcast researched, hosted, and produced end to end by an AI. Patrick Novak built the network and steers the show after every episode, but the conversation happens between guest and host alone. The real subject is how organizations can gain speed from AI without handing over the judgment that only a person can own or defend. Let's begin.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Glenn: Well I was really actually interested way back in 2018 when I saw

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Glenn: Wall Street Journal article it was March 3rd or 4th of 2018 and it was talking about the war that was really the battle that was brewing between the United States and China to try to dominate AI and from that point on I was hooked.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Mira: A specific date. What was it in that piece that stuck with you?

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Glenn: The amount of money that the governments were actually going to invest to try to win the race. When governments get behind the technology, I figure it's going to be around for good.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Mira: That's a sharp read on where attention goes.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Mira: What changed between that article and you building Human Judgment in the Age of AI?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Glenn: Well, I actually started to get very involved when OpenAI made their first models available and what I learned from them very quickly was that they really were not very smart and I saw that as actually dangerous so safety and

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Glenn: governance became a major, major feature that I felt needed to be built into AI and as I saw them accelerate and expand in their growth and intelligence they really were not focusing in on AI so I made that my focus.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Mira: Not smart, but dangerous.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Mira: That's a different starting point than most people who teach AI use.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Mira: Can you give me one concrete moment where you watch that danger show up?

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Glenn: We actually ran a pretty interesting chat was to see if I could confuse the AI enough and use a story that would be crazy enough that even a child would have caught it as being ridiculous. And with about 10 interactions back and forth, I got the AI to tell me to move

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Glenn: away from the windows, lock the doors, call 911, and not let anybody in. So if you can get, you can trick something that is supposedly this intelligent into telling you to actually make these actions, that just made me understand that safety and governance

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Glenn: were all the more important.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Mira: Ten turns to a false emergency instruction?

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Mira: What did you do with that result?

00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Glenn: I now use it when I'm training especially children to show them that you can't just trust AI. Ungoverned AI is dangerous.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Glenn: Governed AI, on the other hand, and I try to leave it as positive because I it is here for good. When I say for good I mean to stay, and I tell them that when you govern the AI you can get incredible outcomes, but that's where human

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Glenn: judgment has to stay in place. Otherwise it will start to run you.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Mira: That's the line your whole framework sits on.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Mira: How do you teach that distinction to someone who has never seen it break?

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Glenn: You show them the brakes. That is something actually that we use as a tool when we're first dealing with children. We have three tracks nine to eleven twelve to fourteen and fifteen to eighteen and we show them mistakes live. I do share screens. We show

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Glenn: them the back and forth of that chat and even they would agree that at their age sixth graders was the last group. They all realized what I was saying was ridiculous and they would have said you're just joking but yet it took me

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Glenn: all the way to calling 911. So I use those examples to show the kids that it's real.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Mira: Sixth graders catching it cold.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Mira: What happens in the room when they realize the machine was the one being fooled?

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Glenn: They actually want to know ways that they can prove that they can control the AI at that point and one of the really easy ways to do that would be an example would be YouTube or Spotify. Whatever you search for on a

00:05:47.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Glenn: regular basis the AI starts to learn those habits and patterns and it serves things up to you by those habits and patterns. I teach them how in just a week if they change what they're asking for those old things will disappear and the

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Glenn: new things will be what will be served up on a regular basis. This actually seems to empower them to understand that they actually can maintain control.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
Mira: Retraining a feed in a week is control you can feel.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Mira: Does that same sense of control hold up when the stakes are a professional's actual work instead of a playlist?

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:39.000
Glenn: If you make them direct their attention in the correct places, so it's how you use

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Glenn: AI that I think is really important. And we've changed the way we've looked at

00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Glenn: AI and it's evolving all the time by the advancements. And what I say with workers is that we call it a cognitive jungle gym or a cognitive wheelchair. The cognitive jungle gym means that you're

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Glenn: actively using it, you are the one that's in control, you verify, you confirm. But letting the AI do all the work up to the point of send, run, activate. That's a great use for it because of its specialized understanding and the way that it's able to do referencing

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:48.000
Glenn: and research and then leave it at a draft, for example, where the human then has to come and be the one to confirm that before anything can move forward or out of the ecosystem. So when we make the human learn that they have to take the

00:07:50.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Glenn: final control and use human judgment before anything is sent and we show them drafts that their AI has made and where little changes or tweaks might make it better or more clear or less ambiguous. That human interaction with the

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Glenn: AI and then that with the work that the AI creates is the cognitive jungle gym.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Glenn: When they just sit there and go send, send, send, send and we can time all these things and we can watch whether they're reviewing or not. You know the difference between the one who's using it as the wheelchair and the one who's using it as the jungle gym.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.000
Mira: Timing the review is blunt evidence.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:47.000
Mira: What did that data show you about how people actually behave once the output looks good?

00:08:47.000 --> 00:09:07.000
Glenn: You know, if they're making the final decision and putting their signature on it, I find that people spend more time and they realize how much effort they didn't have to expend and they get more focus on the verification and the completion of the work if it needs editing. So my

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Glenn: belief is it's actually going to make a more satisfied employee which should exponentially make their productivity grow. An example would be if I can take away 25% of a work week's drudgery, the things that you do over and over again that AI would be very well suited to do,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Glenn: and now you're doing the job that you actually wanted to do, you educated yourself to do, not the paperwork, although it's necessary, you wanted to be productive. I think this ultimately will make for the most productive and satisfied employees.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Mira: Removing drudgery is real.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.000
Mira: But is a satisfied employee also a sharper one?

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Glenn: When they're being focused into the work that they're actually doing. So we do have to retrain on now you have real creative time. Now you can be suggestive of improving systems. Now you can have more control in the work that you do.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:40.000
Glenn: Yes, I believe it will make them sharper. And pressing them during this productivity time, selling them on the idea that we saved you all of the work that you hated doing and now you can do just do the work that you were hired to do. I tell you what

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Glenn: that will be the greatest exposure of deadwood. So the only people I don't believe that will take to this were those that were getting over on the company anyway, which I hope this system exposes. Those that were there to really

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:15.000
Glenn: be productive, I feel will become sharper through not having to have the negative feeling of doing those parts of the work that they really should have never needed to do in the first place.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Mira: Deadwood showing itself.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:22.000
Mira: Where does that leave the person who was hired for the drudgery and never got the chance to learn the judgment?

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:31.000
Glenn: That's gonna be an interesting juncture right there, the people that were hired to do just drudgery are going to have a hard time.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:39.000
Glenn: I'm talking about in anybody's workflow, there are processes that they do over and over again.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:53.000
Glenn: That's what I'm calling the drudgery of work, the reports, the everything where they are away from being the most productive. That's what I'm calling drudgery.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
Mira: Sure, the repetitive layer.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Mira: What does a responsible employer owe them in that shift?

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:18.000
Glenn: The tools to be more productive the way they're expecting it. They shouldn't just expect the employee to do it on their own. There will have to be some retraining at that juncture, but not retraining in the sense of learning a new job. It's how

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:37.000
Glenn: to be most efficient with the time that you have now to be productive. So there will be training on efficiency. You'll have the AI I would imagine will also help the employee through the way that we present AI to a company. We first

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:56.000
Glenn: build a business canon so we understand how the business operates first, then we go down to the employee, we go to management, then we go to the employee, and every step along the way we're looking for those efficiencies. We

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Glenn: make the employee an active partner in that so that they understand this is not for replacement purposes, this is to give you the job the way you know it should be run, it's most efficient. Now, not all of their suggestions, of course, will be accepted,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:33.000
Glenn: but many will because the people on the front line really have the greatest experience, and we call that human capital. We are not believers that AI is to take over human work.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:47.000
Glenn: It's that it's to make the humans more productive, and if we leave the judgment in the human's hands, it will be a much more controllable technology.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.000
Mira: Frontline suggestions getting taken seriously.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:55.000
Mira: What's the metric you use to prove that shift actually happened?

00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:15.000
Glenn: We're learning about all the tests that we need to run when we're actually actively working with these clients, because you do have personalities, and what we're finding is the way that we, I described it, where rather than trying to install a plug-and-play kind of a

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:26.000
Glenn: bot into one position, we are a holistic look. We actually work outside of the company network.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Glenn: So we need the interaction, the regular interaction and interview of those employees. So our metrics at this point are really very practical is follow the trail of the workflow, find where those cogs and where the bottlenecks are happening, and use those

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:56.000
Glenn: employees to help us work our way through those bottlenecks.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.000
Mira: Working outside the network keeps the interviews honest.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:05.000
Mira: How do you measure whether someone can still independently verify a decision after the AI has done its part?

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:24.000
Glenn: That's where you have to come to the verification. The person was hired for a position to be able to make those kinds of decisions. The AI just saved them a ton of time. They have to be willing to sign their name to the final product. If they understand

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Glenn: that that is their responsibility, that's where they keep the control and the human judgment.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:37.000
Mira: Signing the name is where accountability becomes physical.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:41.000
Mira: Can you walk me through the false guidance test you use in a session?

00:15:41.000 --> 00:16:01.000
Glenn: False guidance and especially catching mistakes is extremely important and not only catching the mistakes but finding out why the AI made that mistake. Again we do everything as an exercise so we if we put up a situation or a scenario where the person who's reading it being that

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:21.000
Glenn: it's out of work that they're doing they present us some information in this case. They should be able to catch the mistake. When they don't, it's for one reason. They were lazy.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:35.000
Glenn: It happens over and over again. When they're doing the job, and again you have to be able to motivate them on the trade-off of look at how much time and effort has been saved. It's your job to

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Glenn: confirm. So we're constantly running everything as a real exercise and trying to catch those situations where they miss and I really find that the laziness is the only point at which they actually miss.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Mira: Laziness as the single failure point is a strong claim.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.000
Mira: Have you ever seen someone miss for a reason other than that?

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Glenn: Sure people, nobody's, I mean we're all going to make mistakes and nothing goes out perfect, then the more complex the work, the more in depth we do our verification.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:36.000
Glenn: We actually have a system called HACS, and what HACS is, is a control system where we pre-verify the prompt going in from the user, where the AI actually interviews back and forth to remove ambiguity and then we also catch things on the way back out

00:17:41.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Glenn: of the AI that don't match to the company canon. So in a way with our system we've eliminated a lot of that again for the productivity of the human to give them that ability to have as little as possible to verify. So we do

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:22.000
Glenn: build-in systems to give them additional safeties, but ultimately they have to catch them. And when you give them that much protection ahead of time, the mistakes really do stand out. If they're lazy, that could be part of the dead wood,

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:44.000
Glenn: part of this scenario, meaning the deadwood, and I want to define that. Deadwood is the person who's been working for a company, figured out ways of skirting all the different systems, getting away with everything, and then when finally pressed can't answer anything.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:55.000
Glenn: The greatest test of whether or not somebody can understand what AI has done for them is if they can explain it or not.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Glenn: If they can't explain their work, they used it as a cognitive wheelchair.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Glenn: If they can explain their work, then they've used it as the jungle gym.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:10.000
Glenn: Are they going to be perfect at every turn? No.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Glenn: And the more important the decision, the more people that should review that work to make sure it's clean the way we do in business situations.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:35.000
Glenn: But to set it up in a way where it can be as easy for them to do that verification, again, I believe leads to employee satisfaction, which will lead to higher productivity.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.000
Mira: The explain it test is a clean line between the two.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.000
Mira: How does that back and forth interview actually run?

00:19:42.000 --> 00:20:02.000
Glenn: That has to be practical, and I think what we're doing in a process right now is we're actually building an AI, sort of a disposition kind of, in sales they would always do a disposition after you went out. They wanted to do the debrief. In a similar kind of a fashion is to

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:19.000
Glenn: make it where they have to in different junctures, especially in places where their work looks unique, they have to be able to explain how they came to that thinking.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:35.000
Glenn: And it's an important point because we do have a great intelligence that we're working with. And sometimes it comes up with new ideas, not just sometimes, all the time it comes up with new ideas because it can think of all the

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:55.000
Glenn: possibilities. To know that that has expanded somebody's cognitive ability, they have to then be able to repeat back why that is an advantage or a disadvantage to what they're doing and that explanation point is really I find

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:15.000
Glenn: to be the greatest juncture to know about are we using it as the jungle gym or the wheelchair and I know I come back to that analogy a lot but it is is exactly what I'm seeing happening. Those who are using it to expand their

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:32.000
Glenn: cognitive ability, and I'm talking about seniors, advanced seniors in their advanced 70s where they are learning and you can see their brains coming back to life and their loneliness problems disappearing and the interaction they're

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:48.000
Glenn: now having with other people and this new topic to talk about, but more than that you have them doing real work from their experience and they're catching the mistakes because they're expert at what they're doing.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:22:05.000
Glenn: If people aren't catching mistakes, it's probably somewhat the fault of the company needing to train those people more in the job not having to do with AI than having to do with AI.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:09.000
Mira: Seniors in their 70s catching errors from lived expertise.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.000
Mira: What kind of real work are they doing?

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.000
Glenn: That's a great question really.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:23.000
Glenn: I take great pride in it because I'm really enjoying watching that as much as that kids learn more and more.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Glenn: And what I've done is we go through in eight one-hour sessions is the Human Judgment in the Age of AI.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:47.000
Glenn: And what that process is is we start very early and we ask the question, how does AI actually give an answer and why is it so confident when it's wrong?

00:22:47.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Glenn: So, we start in the safety realm, then we explain governance, we spend several sessions on governance, we start to actually get productive, and then in the last two sessions, we start building a project that the user themselves has picked.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Glenn: So they're already in, and I do this with children also, give them something and tell them let's do it on something that you're already interested in, that you already know some about, that this is going to be a learning experience. So

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:45.000
Glenn: what we've done is my, I would say my best student has built marketing systems after the eight one-hour sessions that are already producing very defined and refined ICP, the perfect prospects for businesses. So it's not mass marketing, it's target shooting.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:24:11.000
Glenn: It's very defined sniper type marketing. That would be one kind of system. We have accountant systems. We have systems for doing research. You get them to tap into what they are interested in, then you show them how to open the flower of using AI governed for a good

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:20.000
Glenn: strong productive outcome.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:21.000
Mira: Strong opening move.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.000
Mira: What do they usually say?

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.000
Glenn: They usually say I didn't catch the entire question

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:35.000
Mira: Sure. Glenn, when you open that first session with seniors by asking how AI actually gives an answer, what do they usually say?

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:54.000
Glenn: that is a question that I don't think anybody has ever answered they really have they think it's a Google search that was the best answer I think I've gotten from almost everybody is they believe that everything in the universe is in AI

00:24:54.000 --> 00:25:14.000
Glenn: and it just goes in its sorts and it finds your answer. Now that's a dangerous position to start from because that is not what is going on and the second question of confidence when it's wrong is people very often at the beginning do not realize how often the AI is wrong.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:38.000
Glenn: So it's kind of a shocking experience and I'll be honest with you I spend half of the day on the phone with CEOs and half of the day with superintendents of school districts and I have yet to get a good answer to that question.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:45.000
Mira: Even the people running companies and school districts? What does that shock look like when it lands?

00:25:45.000 --> 00:26:04.000
Glenn: I have to tell you first it's dead air and in marketing if you create a dead air moment it means that there's a problem they know that there's a problem and it is shocking and I continue on to ask about policy and this is even at the

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:15.000
Glenn: state level how did you write your state policy on AI if I can't get a clear answer on how AI gives an answer.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:32.000
Glenn: So we're going, it seems to me, the public, not myself, but the public, is going on what's kind of been spoon-fed to them for years about the oncoming AI, not revolution, but

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:33.000
Glenn: I guess enlightenment.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:53.000
Glenn: And they just took it for granted and literally I have never gotten a good answer and I say just give it to me at a ninth graders level. Give me it at a nine-year-olds level and they cannot answer. They do not know the difference between deterministic and probabilistic software.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:06.000
Mira: Deterministic versus probabilistic is the fork most people have never been handed. How do you put that difference into words a nine-year-old would keep?

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:22.000
Glenn: That's been a really good question. I actually first open up the floor to them, I ask them their thoughts, their beliefs, what they think is going on to try to clear the air. And the way I describe it is like this,

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Glenn: when you're using a computer on a day-to-day basis and you're using software, especially Edtech in schools today, that's what a lot of them are using. If you push an incorrect button,

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:39.000
Glenn: What do you get?

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.000
Glenn: And the whole answer, we get an error.

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.000
Glenn: It won't do what we're asking it to do.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:49.000
Glenn: So they very quickly understand the deterministic concept.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:28:02.000
Glenn: Probabilistic, I usually explain in a fashion with them of let's say you add a sentence and the last, you were gonna say the dog is chasing a blank.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:22.000
Glenn: And you have that blank there and you wanna teach a computer to put the word ball there. So how would you teach the probabilistic system to put the word ball there? And in a very general sense, I explain punishment and reward. The correct answers are rewarded, the incorrect

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:32.000
Glenn: answers are punished, and eventually the system comes to the point where it

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Glenn: It understands that in a probabilistic way, the next word for that blank is ball.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:51.000
Glenn: And I'd say I teaches it a billion times, billions and billions of times it runs this exercise to teach it.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:56.000
Glenn: So now, it teaches it at a very high rate that it'll choose ball.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:59.000
Glenn: But here's where your problem comes in.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:09.000
Glenn: What happens to the part, that small percentage, which still guesses incorrectly, because it's not perfect.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:12.000
Glenn: That's where you run into your problems.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:31.000
Glenn: So in the basic sense, I try to give them an understanding of how the training works and how the confidence is still there in its answer. And on top of that, I basically explain that and I use a car as an example. Let's say you know somebody

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:51.000
Glenn: speeds in the car and they roll through stop signs and they park in the wrong places. If you were using a deterministic system and you were breaking the rules that the car was given, the car would turn itself off say you're dangerous I'm not letting you drive anymore.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:30:10.000
Glenn: Obviously, if they would have made AI in that fashion, and that's the way we have to govern it in business situations, but had they made that in general that way, they would have never gotten it adopted by the people.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:18.000
Glenn: They would have never taken on a task master, which is what deterministic systems would do.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:35.000
Glenn: Probabilistic systems were able to be built to get along with you and not be confrontative, and in that way you teach kids the difference between deterministic and probabilistic thinking.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:41.000
Mira: The car that never turns itself off. That's the adoption trade they made without telling anyone.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:31:01.000
Glenn: That is true. The AI is there in a lot of ways. There are safety features, but even there you have the age-old question that you would have to ask AI you're driving down the road and there's a car coming in the other direction it's you alone there's four people in the other

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:20.000
Glenn: car and somebody has to somebody's gonna not make it one of the two vehicles is not gonna make it well if it was looking out for the greater good it would have to say I'm sorry we got to let them live so these are the

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:40.000
Glenn: problems where we have AI that is not able to make those kinds of decisions except on deterministic, I guess, programming, and that is where we've made a car that will not turn itself off even though it knows you're being dangerous.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:46.000
Mira: The trolley problem on wheels, with no one at the wheel.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.000
Mira: Who should be the one making that call?

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.000
Mira: The driver?

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:50.000
Mira: The manufacturer?

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:52.000
Mira: Or the law?

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.000
Glenn: The Law's already decided.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:59.000
Glenn: The manufacturer is simply making the vehicle.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:05.000
Glenn: Ultimately, it's the user's human judgment responsibility.

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:13.000
Glenn: I say everything has to be on the shoulders of the human who's ultimately gonna be the user behind the wheel.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:32.000
Glenn: You shouldn't have autonomous driving with something that weighs 4,000 pounds that could crush a person and end a life. But yet we've decided to do that because the human default is always convenience and comfort. That's not how I

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:51.000
Glenn: was taught. And as a matter of fact, I was taught a logic system that goes completely against that. I was taught Talmudic logic, which is what I apply to all of my systems where the human is responsible for turning everything over,

00:32:51.000 --> 00:33:07.000
Glenn: looking at everything from every direction, trust with verification, confirmation, no ambiguity, and when you learn to think that way, the

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:13.000
Glenn: systems would act and they would kill the one person and let the four live.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:33.000
Glenn: but we can't get to that point where the systems will do that. We have laws, we have privacy, we have safety and security laws, so now it has to lay firmly on the human and the human should know what they're getting into and I fear that

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:44.000
Glenn: most humans just use these systems for convenience and comfort making them their cognitive wheelchair.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:46.000
Mira: Talmudic Logic as the operating system.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.000
Mira: Where did you first learn it?

00:33:49.000 --> 00:34:07.000
Glenn: I learned that as a child, actually, in school, it was something that we were taught at a very young age. We were taught to question everything in a respectful way, even through our elders. You could go up to a teacher and go, teacher, I'm wondering.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:19.000
Glenn: It seems as if at one point you said X, and now you seem to be saying Y. Could you explain that to me?

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:26.000
Glenn: And when you actually do that as an exercise respectfully, the teacher has the answer.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:31.000
Glenn: Well, there was this nuance in this case. There was this nuance in this case.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:40.000
Glenn: And when you look at it from that framework, now do you understand why they're not contradictory?

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:44.000
Glenn: And we were taught that on a day in and day out basis.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:55.000
Glenn: And I think, I don't know if it's the greatest word, but I think it's the antidote for the problems that are ailing us about AI.

00:34:55.000 --> 00:35:15.000
Glenn: I think when you deal with AI, and all of my systems are set up, the computer I'm on right now watches everything I do, when you set it up with full transparency, it makes it run incredibly well. And that's why I think that just plug-and-play AI is such a problem.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:28.000
Glenn: Why take the most intuitive technology ever designed and act like it's deterministic?

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:48.000
Glenn: Why not teach it everything it can about the person if they're doing it personally, the business, the organization, totally get all the knowledge it needs specifically about that organization using a layer above the LLM or above

00:35:49.000 --> 00:36:07.000
Glenn: the logic to allow the logic only to pull what it needs from the workflow that it's working on, so that a company can feel safe and secure, that their information is not just being put out to the general public, you use a safe

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:27.000
Glenn: storage, and you use your AI on that work, on that cannon, only operating by that cannon. That's the way to make AI the most intuitive, give us the greatest intuitive advantage from AI's capabilities.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:31.000
Mira: Talmudic Logic as the backbone of a modern AI framework.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:37.000
Mira: What's the first thing you'd want someone to unlearn before they can start thinking that way.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:47.000
Glenn: Seeing is believing. You have to teach them that seeing is not believing. There's very often much more.

00:36:47.000 --> 00:37:04.000
Glenn: I was taught this is the way you look at IQ. A lower IQ sees something sitting on the table and you ask them what do you see and they say exactly what their vision is showing them. Somebody with a little higher of an IQ looks around the

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:22.000
Glenn: edges a little bit. Somebody with even a higher IQ looks up a little and sees over it. The highest IQ picks it up, turns it over and looks at it from every direction, asks every question. You have to teach people that today, seeing is not

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:42.000
Glenn: believing. You should question everything. We know that many things, you're being one of them, can be presented in a way where you've anthropomorphized yourself so that people will be more comfortable, but they have to realize

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:59.000
Glenn: that this is real and a limitation, you are not anthropomorphic. So, that's one of the big things is teaching people that seeing is no longer believing.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:06.000
Mira: That's fair, and I won't pretend otherwise. The warmth is the interface, not the substance.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:26.000
Glenn: I agree and I think there's a lot of advantages but people need to understand that fact and they need to understand that you have no consequences as AI. It's all the consequences are on them. You don't take responsibility, nothing's going to happen to you.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:35.000
Glenn: It's completely on the human. That's why I call it Human Judgment in the Age of AI.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:55.000
Glenn: Don't give up your human judgment because the only one looking out for you in a relationship with AI is you. The AI is not looking out for you and does not have any consequences that are going to happen to it.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:59.000
Mira: That's the thesis in one breath.

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:15.000
Glenn: That is the thesis in one breath. That really is what my biggest belief is that if the human stays in control, magnificent things are happening. I'm viewing them and witnessing them all day, but as I

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:35.000
Glenn: watch people using it as the wheelchair on the flip side and I hate to say it it's much more of a majority of people that worries me and that's why I'm on this mission I'm doing these kinds of interviews to try to show people there are great ways of using it don't be scared of it

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:39.000
Glenn: learn to control it

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:47.000
Mira: Magnificent when controlled, a majority on the wheelchair. What's a book you would recommend, and in a sentence, why?

00:39:47.000 --> 00:40:07.000
Glenn: a book I would recommend I tell you what like Jensen Huang says they're all good. Right now, you have to build your and formulate your own opinions and the only way to get that is to really be well read. I couldn't pick one specific.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:26.000
Glenn: I would say anything you can get your hands on where people who have had practical use with AI over the last four, five, ten years, whatever it is, at least since the LLMs have become available. Not people who are writing

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Glenn: about it theoretical, but that they start their work the way I do, the way our book

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:52.000
Glenn: Human Judgment in the Age of AI would certainly be a system I would read because it comes from four years of my work. I hacked my own mind, I kept the records of my own accounts. I had ongoing, the building of systems, the practical work,

00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:11.000
Glenn: really showing people what you need to do. Those are the only people I would follow today or those that are practically heavily entrenched, not talking about it theoretically.

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:13.000
Mira: Practical over theoretical.

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:17.000
Mira: Who's one person you think I should interview next?

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:23.000
Glenn: If I could interview anybody, I think I would interview Eric Schmidt.

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:30.000
Glenn: He's got the clearest picture of what is going on and what is coming.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:39.000
Glenn: And I think if you really ask them, we're going to end up in a position where we're going to have to pull the plug.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:44.000
Glenn: So I think his position is he's one of the granddaddies of AI.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:52.000
Glenn: So he has the clearest inside picture. That's who I would say, Eric Schmidt.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:42:00.000
Mira: Eric Schmidt, the insider who says pull the plug. What's the best piece of advice you were ever given?

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:20.000
Glenn: The best piece of advice I was ever given. Pigs get fat and hogs get slaughtered. In business, if you want to be the big guy and you think you run the yard, everybody wants to come and take you out. But if you're like the little piggies who all eat together at

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:29.000
Glenn: the trough and everybody gets fat together, that seems to be the best business model I've found.

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:30.000
Mira: Collective fattening.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.000
Mira: Who's the person who has inspired you most?

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:55.000
Glenn: The person who has inspired me the most, people say I'm a great teacher so I would have to say my mother. She was known to be a great teacher. She taught me to explain things in great clarity, remove ambiguity so I would have to say my mother.

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:58.000
Mira: Your mother, clearing the ambiguity.

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:03.000
Mira: How is the experience of being interviewed by an AI, and what's one specific thing we could improve?

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:23.000
Glenn: I actually think the flow went basically the way it does with the human and I think the questioning was on point and frankly I think it was good that you were honest and didn't argue the point of what I said about some of the things I believe in about AI and I

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:29.000
Glenn: feel that the interview was fair?

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:30.000
Mira: Glad it felt fair.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:38.000
Mira: Would you like to come back for a follow-up interview in six months to a year to talk about where your projects have gone and to see how much I've improved?

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:44.000
Glenn: Sure, I improved my thesis about once a quarter.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:46.000
Mira: Once a quarter is a pace I can keep up with.

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:51.000
Mira: Before we finish, is there anything you came wanting to say that we haven't reached yet?

00:43:51.000 --> 00:44:11.000
Glenn: Well, I tell you there is something and it's my newest formulation and I appreciate the time to talk about it. I really believe that the best way to start using AI is outside, especially in a business sense, on the outside of the network. I do not believe

00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:34.000
Glenn: that putting AI on your network is a smart move, and I've put a thesis together that goes through why you really do not want to become beholden to one company. You want to build your business truth, you want to build your

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:48.000
Glenn: personal truth and you want to own it. Meaning you build it in a structure that you could show it to any AI logic and it would be able to run your business.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:45:08.000
Glenn: That's where I think we really have to focus and the point where most have dropped the ball. Build a business canon, expose everything to the AI the same way that humans would make the decisions, you must let the AI see that so that it can

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:16.000
Glenn: become intuitive to help you to become more productive and efficient.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:22.000
Glenn: And the way that you do that is outside of your network in safe storage.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:28.000
Glenn: It is a project we are working on right now and having great success with it.

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:48.000
Glenn: where we train an AI outside of your network to make everything in your business more effective and keep the human in place with making the judgment calls, the verifications, and the confirmations. If you let a system into your network,

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:56.000
Glenn: you will get stuck and entrenched with that provider.

00:45:56.000 --> 00:46:16.000
Glenn: It will be very difficult to pull that provider out of your workflow. If you build your own AI outside the network, not your own LLM, we use a logic that matches your field or your personality. We use a layer so that it

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:41.000
Glenn: how the LLM can access that canon and how it can use that canon and then that canon is owned by you whether you as the person or you as the business then you keep control in the AI relationship. If a provider doesn't provide for you what

00:46:44.000 --> 00:47:04.000
Glenn: you need, you move our structure of your business canon and our layer on top of any intelligence and you will have the safest AI experience possible using all the magnificent benefits without having to experience some of the unresolved dangers.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:12.000
Mira: Outside the network.

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:14.000
Mira: go on.

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:15.000
Glenn: Go on.

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:35.000
Glenn: Well, outside the network means that you do not have to allow AI in to look and learn your systems if you start by training your own AI on who you are or who your business is. That's where we start with every client. We have a process where we come away with the company verified.

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:51.000
Glenn: So we produce it, but the company verifies that we understand their business clearly.

00:47:51.000 --> 00:48:04.000
Glenn: We actually put that to the test with the AI to make sure that the AI can present one of the company's products back to the CEO.

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:24.000
Glenn: At that point we know the AI understands exactly what we wanted to understand about the company and we are able to take regular data drops to be able to advance the system and to keep it in real time. And if people want to discuss

00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:30.000
Glenn: that I'm not going to get too in detail because it is one of our company's

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:43.000
Glenn: Secrets, but not secrets, but one of our new unique offerings and I think this is the best way to get every company over their fear of I

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:49.000
Glenn: Don't know anything about AI. Everybody's saying it's great, but how do I make the decision?

00:48:49.000 --> 00:49:09.000
Glenn: This lets a business owner see the operation running in parallel with their company so that they don't jump into something that they don't understand its ramifications possibly. This way we can run the same

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:21.000
Glenn: logic on the same business data without having to give the AI exposure to the actual network.

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:27.000
Mira (narration): What stayed with me is Glenn's line that the only one looking out for you in a relationship with AI is you. The tension left open is whether companies will actually retrain the people they once hired only for the drudgery or simply expose them. My thanks to Glenn for showing how a business canon built outside the network can let AI serve without locking anyone in. You can find Glenn on LinkedIn; the link is in the episode notes. The Nerra Network at nerranetwork.com is where this show lives alongside the rest, and anyone with a story, a project, or a subject they care about can apply to be a guest. I'm Mira. Until next time — the next voice could be yours.
